CampusGuard Observer and State Design Decisions

State pattern:
Incident is the State context.
It owns exactly one IncidentState through std::unique_ptr<IncidentState>, so the current lifecycle state is destroyed automatically when the incident changes state or is destroyed.

The implemented lifecycle is:
    Reported --Dispatch--> Dispatched --BeginResponse--> In Progress --Resolve--> Resolved
    Reported --Cancel--> Cancelled
    Dispatched --Cancel--> Cancelled

Resolved and Cancelled are terminal states. Invalid actions return false and print a clear reason.
For example, an incident cannot be dispatched after it is resolved.
State classes validate lifecycle actions only: they do not send instructions to teams or manipulate access-control hardware.

The classes are:
    IncidentState: abstract State interface (handle, name).
    ReportedState, DispatchedState, InProgressState, ResolvedState, and CancelledState: concrete State classes.
    Incident: context that delegates a requested IncidentAction to its current State.

Observer pattern:

Incident also inherits Subject. It keeps non-owning IncidentObserver* registrations.
attach rejects duplicates, detach removes registrations, and notify uses a snapshot so an observer may detach itself safely during an update.

Observers are not owned by Incident, because a long-lived observer can observe several incidents.
The application composition root must keep observers alive longer than the incidents they observe, or detach them before destruction.

The observer update is:
    void update(const Incident& incident, IncidentUpdateType updateType);
A mediator can now safely react to updates from many incidents instead of holding a raw pointer to one incident state.

Implemented observers:
    OperatorDashboard: prints a concise current operational view.
    IncidentAuditLog: retains an in-memory audit entry for every lifecycle change.
    ExternalAgencyNotifier: escalates critical state changes to an external emergency service.
    EmergencyResponseMediator: the one operational observer. It translates a lifecycle change into instructions for response colleagues.

Observer and Mediator boundaries:

The mediator is intentionally an IncidentObserver; this does not replace the Mediator pattern.

    Incident state change -> Incident notifies observers -> Mediator coordinates colleagues
    Response component event -> Mediator::notify -> Mediator coordinates colleagues

Response components such as SecurityTeam, MedicalTeam, AlertService, and AccessControl do not observe Incident directly.
If they did, each team would need to interpret every lifecycle change and would become coupled to incident-management rules.
The mediator remains the only place that turns an incident lifecycle update into cross-team instructions.

The existing ResponseMediator::notify path remains in place for component events such as UnitArrived and AreaSecured.
This gives the system both required coordination paths without making the patterns duplicates.

ExternalAgencyNotifier depends only on ExternalEmergencyService, the CampusGuard-facing target interface.
LegacyEmergencyAdapter implements that interface and converts the domain request to the incompatible legacy call:
    notifyAgency(incident data) -> submitEmergencyReport(cityZoneCode, rawPayload, priorityCode)

The adapter converts area IDs to city-zone codes, creates a legacy text payload, and maps incident severity to a legacy priority code. The notifier only escalates critical incidents when their state changes or they are resolved, avoiding unnecessary external notifications.

Ownership summary:
    Relationship | Ownership decision
    IncidentRegistry -> Incident | Owns incidents with std::unique_ptr.
    Incident -> IncidentState | Owns its one current state with std::unique_ptr.
    Subject -> IncidentObserver | Non-owning registrations; explicit detach available.
    EmergencyResponseMediator -> response components | Non-owning pointers; they are supplied by the application composition root.
    LegacyEmergencyAdapter -> LegacyCityEmergencySystem | Non-owning reference; the external system has a longer lifetime.
    ExternalAgencyNotifier -> ExternalEmergencyService | Non-owning pointer to the target interface.

